      "math_academy_id": "e-13149",
      "problem": "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$",
